WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:01.970
Next question today.

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:07.500
David, if Trump does delay deal the election,
can you tell me definitively who becomes president?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.870
I've heard Nancy Pelosi.

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.120
I've heard Chuck Grassley.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.379
I don't really understand.

00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:13.880
OK, so this came up briefly in a voicemail
earlier this week.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.950
But let's go through it again.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:22.310
If Donald Trump delays the election enough
and again, I'm not debating here whether he

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.570
can do it, how it could be done, how much
he would do.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.840
I don't want to do that stuff because there's
all sorts.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.650
You know, the electors have to vote.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.750
I believe it's in the first half of December.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.160
And there's other dates and elections are
run by the states.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.340
These are 50 state decisions more than they
are anything that Donald Trump would do.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.000
So let's skip that piece for now.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.510
But let's imagine we get to January 20th and
there has not been an election.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.670
Trump doesn't stay in power.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.510
Trump stops being the president.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.850
Now, when you look at the line of succession.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.850
OK.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.330
It would go from president to vice president.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.040
But Mike Pence also would no longer be vice
president.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.270
Well, then it would go to the speaker of the
House, Nancy Pelosi.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:10.579
Well, if the election in California doesn't
happen, she is no longer elected.

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:13.329
So she's no longer speaker of the House.

00:01:13.329 --> 00:01:14.830
So it would skip her.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.729
So eventually it would go to the Senate pro
tem.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:24.490
Now, currently, the Senate pro tem is Chuck
Grassley, a Republican, because Republicans

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.190
have the majority in the Senate.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:33.159
But the problem is that 35 Senate seats are
up for election in November.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.939
Of the hundred Senate seats.

00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:40.580
If those 35 elections don't happen again,
we're assuming none of the elections happen.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.679
You might have some states do the election,
but not enough to select the president.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:47.030
But let's skip that for now.

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:54.190
If those 35 Senate seats end up being open,
you now only have 65 senators.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.749
And that means that the Democrats would have
a majority because Republicans have far more

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:01.039
seats to defend in November than do Democrats.

00:02:01.039 --> 00:02:05.490
So if none of these 35 elections happen, Republicans
lose more seats than Democrats.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.690
You then have no president, no vice president,
no House.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.540
But you have a Senate made up of the 65 remaining
senators who are more than 50 percent Democrats,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:20.200
meaning Democrats get to select a Senate pro
tem who becomes the president until this is

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.200
resolved.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.610
Now, historically, again, this is our best
understanding producer.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.711
And I have been researching this.

00:02:26.711 --> 00:02:28.690
This is our best understanding.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:35.210
Historically, Senate pro tem tends to go to
the most senior senator from the party right

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.210
now.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.180
That's Vermont's Patrick Leahy.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:45.480
However, it's conceivable that if the 65 remaining
senators knew that by electing a Senate pro

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.560
tem, they were essentially selecting the next
president.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.280
It's conceivable they would pick someone other
than Patrick Leahy.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.570
But that's the scenario, as I best understand
it today.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.290
That would determine who is president.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:01.600
If we don't have a presidential election in
November.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.400
Now, let me go back to what I said when I
commented on James Clyburn earlier this week,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:11.100
saying, I think Trump is going to, after he
loses, use some kind of emergency to stay

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.480
in power.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.610
I believe that our coverage of that possibility
needs to focus around the idea that the mere

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:24.420
suggestion of delaying a federal election,
which has never happened, is a despotic, authoritarian,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.100
dystopian nightmare.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:32.140
The now that we I was asked the question,
what exactly would happen?

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.010
I've answered it to the best of my ability.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.800
I don't think that's where the focus should
be.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.570
The focus should be that Trump even floating
this idea is completely outrageous.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.590
We vote in this country.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.480
We figure out how to do it.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:51.190
States are expanding absentee ballot access,
universal absentee voting, as I'm calling

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.660
it.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.480
And we're going to have an election.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.500
And Donald Trump is going to either win or
not win and things are going to happen on

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.790
the schedule they're supposed.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.520
That has to be the default conversation once
you start engaging in what would justify delaying

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.030
it or what would be the mechanism for delaying
it.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:13.090
Academically, it's interesting, but it's problematic
when it becomes a corporate media dialog,

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:15.030
which only legitimizes the idea at its core.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:15.760
So that that's my take on that.

